From f57aa94590f7f3f8da46086b5bbca92f377d4897 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Dawid=20Wr=C3=B3bel?= <me@dawidwrobel.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 02:42:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 09/41] tee: qseecom: require CAP_SYS_ADMIN to open the
 privileged device

The privileged device loads code into the secure world and registers the
services trusted applications call back into. The TEE core separates it from
the client device by minor number and reports TEE_GEN_CAP_PRIVILEGED, but
enforces nothing, so until now the entire guard was whatever mode and owner
created the node -- and unprivileged access to this interface's character
device has been an escalation step before now (CVE-2018-9411).

Require the capability in the open path, so the guard does not depend on a
distribution getting a udev rule right.

Deliberately not applied to the client device. A daemon that talks to one
trusted application has no business holding CAP_SYS_ADMIN, so that one stays a
matter of file permissions and LSM policy, which the documentation now says
rather than leaving "restrict it" as advice with no mechanism behind it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
---
 Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst | 19 ++++++++++++++-----
 drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c    | 12 ++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 26 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst b/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst
index c0602cd83..dafd85f83 100644
--- a/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst
+++ b/Documentation/tee/qseecom.rst
@@ -111,8 +111,17 @@ quiet corner.
 
 Access to the device nodes is therefore part of the security boundary rather
 than a packaging detail -- reaching the old character device from an
-unprivileged context was itself an escalation step (CVE-2018-9411). Restrict
-them deliberately rather than leaving a permissive default: the privileged
-device to root, since it loads applications and registers listeners, and the
-client device to whichever service requires it. Widening them turns a trustlet
-bug into local privilege escalation.
+unprivileged context was itself an escalation step (CVE-2018-9411).
+
+The privileged device requires ``CAP_SYS_ADMIN`` to open. It loads code into
+the secure world and registers the services trusted applications call back
+into, which is not something to gate on file permissions alone; the TEE core
+separates it from the client device by minor number and reports
+``TEE_GEN_CAP_PRIVILEGED``, but enforces nothing itself.
+
+No such check is made on the client device, because a fingerprint or
+attestation daemon has no business running with ``CAP_SYS_ADMIN``. Guarding it
+is discretionary and mandatory access control instead: give it to the one
+service that needs it rather than leaving a permissive default, and label it in
+whatever LSM policy the system uses. Widening it turns a trustlet bug into
+local privilege escalation.
diff --git a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
index 28066577e..a0ebf5a27 100644
--- a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
+++ b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@
 
 #define pr_fmt(fmt) KBUILD_MODNAME ": " fmt
 
+#include <linux/capability.h>
 #include <linux/cleanup.h>
 #include <linux/errno.h>
 #include <linux/list.h>
@@ -1036,6 +1037,17 @@ static int qseecom_tee_supp_open(struct tee_context *ctx)
 	struct qseecom_tee *qtee = tee_get_drvdata(ctx->teedev);
 	int ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * This device loads code into the secure world and registers the
+	 * services trusted applications call back into, so it is not something
+	 * to hand out on file permissions alone. The TEE core separates the
+	 * privileged device from the client one by minor number and says so in
+	 * TEE_IOC_VERSION, but enforces nothing itself, which leaves the whole
+	 * guard resting on whatever created the node.
+	 */
+	if (!capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN))
+		return -EPERM;
+
 	ret = qseecom_tee_open(ctx);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
